/* SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0 */
/*
 * Orange Pi 5 Plus Linux + StarryOS + Zephyr three-guest functional image
 * (`zephyr-triple-guest-functional`): pin the guest RAM window to the region the
 * VM configuration actually maps.
 *
 * The Zephyr client (VM3) is given
 * `memory_regions = [[0x4000_0000, 0x0800_0000, 0x7, 0]]` (128 MiB), while the
 * board devicetree declares `sram0` as `memory@40000000` with 1 GiB. Zephyr
 * derives its memory and heap layout from that node, so the compiled devicetree
 * has to describe the same 128 MiB the guest is allowed to touch; otherwise the
 * guest can place data outside its mapped RAM and stall without any CPU fault.
 *
 * The GIC is deliberately *not* overridden. VM3 is a fully virtualized guest
 * with no physical passthrough, and its VM configuration opts in to the
 * synthetic vGIC following the host firmware GIC addresses, so AxVisor serves
 * GICD 0xfe600000 (0x10000) and GICR 0xfe680000 (0x100000) - exactly what the
 * stock RK3588 devicetree already describes in `&gic`. Rewriting `&gic` here
 * would point the guest at the machine-fallback geometry (0x08000000) instead.
 *
 * The UART, the timer PPIs, the entry point and the single vCPU are untouched.
 *
 * Zephyr picks this file up automatically because it is the application overlay
 * named after the board: cmake/modules/configuration_files.cmake turns
 * `<app>/boards/<board>[_<soc>].overlay` into DTC_OVERLAY_FILE. This board has a
 * single SoC, so `orangepi_5_plus_rk3588.overlay` is the shortened name Zephyr
 * accepts; `orangepi_5_plus_rk3588_rk3588.overlay` is the equivalent long name,
 * and Zephyr would fail on conflicting overlay candidates if both existed.
 */
&sram0 {
	reg = <0x40000000 0x08000000>;	/* 0x0800_0000 = 128 MiB guest RAM */
};
